-- #1111 A-2.10 VALID-PROGRAMS BATTERY — the mirror that catches the REGRESSION,
-- as opposed to the one that proves the feature.
--
-- A-2.10 makes every "are these the same type?" test read the module identity
-- Stage A-1 put on a type head.  Making that test STRICTER turns `exit 0` into
-- `exit 1` — which is the fix for two genuinely-distinct types (#1208/#1209) and
-- a REGRESSION for a valid program whose identity SUPPLY is incomplete.  Unit
-- A-2.2b hit exactly that: keying on identity naively made the compiler reject
-- its own prelude (`No impl of Ord for Int`), because `stdlib/runtime.mdk`'s
-- extern signatures never pass through the stamping walk and so arrive with no
-- identity at all.  Every binding below is a shape whose two sides acquire
-- their identity by DIFFERENT routes; each must still infer.
--
--   * `externCmp`    — an EXTERN-sourced `Int` (no identity, `externSchemes`)
--                      meeting the `OriginBuiltin` `Int` the prelude's `Ord`
--                      impl is written against.  This is A-2.2b's own minimal
--                      discriminator, and the single most load-bearing line
--                      here: it is the one a "compare the origins too" rule
--                      rejects.
--   * `externIntoUser` — the same identity-less `Int` flowing into a slot of a
--                      cross-module USER type, so the absence has to survive a
--                      constructor application rather than just a comparison.
--   * `viaRelay` / `viaAlias` — a type and an alias reaching this module across
--                      a RE-EXPORT hop, compared against the definer's own use
--                      of them inside `base.mdk`.  A re-export attributed to
--                      the re-exporter instead of the definer shows up here.
--   * `dispatched`   — an interface declared in `base` with its impl also in
--                      `base`, dispatched from here: the impl head and the goal
--                      head must agree on identity across two hops.
--   * `opt`/`res`/`ord` — PRELUDE types (`OriginModule "core"`), which reach a
--                      user module by a third route again (the prelude is
--                      prepended, not imported).
--   * `pair`/`lst`   — BUILTIN heads (`OriginBuiltin`, §8 I6.2(a)), including
--                      the `__tupleN__` head, which has its own mint.
import relay.{Crate, unCrate, twice, wrap, Boxed}

externCmp : String -> Bool
externCmp s = stringLength s < 2

externIntoUser : String -> Crate Int
externIntoUser s = wrap (stringLength s)

viaRelay : Int
viaRelay = twice (wrap 4)

viaAlias : Boxed -> Int
viaAlias c = unCrate c

dispatched : Int
dispatched = unCrate (wrap 7)

opt : Option Int
opt = Some 3

res : Result String Int
res = Ok 1

ord : Ordering
ord = compare 1 2

pair : (Int, String)
pair = (1, "x")

lst : List Int
lst = [1, 2, 3]
